lesson.example:
Example target: https://target.example/api/v1/docs/swagger-config
Vulnerable code (pseudo‑Java Spring):
@Bean
public Docket api() {
    return new Docket(DocumentationType.SWAGGER_2)
        .select()
        .apis(RequestHandlerSelectors.any())
        .paths(PathSelectors.any())
        .build();
}
// No security filter is applied to "/api/v1/docs/**"

Attack request (curl):
curl -H "Host: internal.target.example" https://target.example/api/v1/docs/swagger-config

Why it works: The Swagger endpoint is exposed without authentication, and the server accepts any Host header, so the attacker receives the full OpenAPI spec containing internal routes.
The response includes endpoints like /admin/login, /internal/db/backup, and a list of user IDs, giving the attacker a blueprint for further exploitation.

lesson.fix:
Restrict the Swagger documentation to internal networks only. In a Spring Boot app, bind the Swagger UI to localhost and protect it with HTTP Basic or OAuth2:
@Configuration
public class SwaggerSecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.authorizeRequests()
            .antMatchers("/api/v*/api-docs/**").authenticated()
            .and()
            .httpBasic();
        return http.build();
    }
}
Additionally, configure the web server or load balancer to allow the IP ranges of the internal network (e.g., 10.0.0.0/8) and block all other source IPs. Ensure the service binds to 127.0.0.1 or the internal interface instead of 0.0.0.0. These steps prevent unauthenticated external users from retrieving the internal OpenAPI specification.

To fix this, you must disable directory indexing in your web server configuration. For an Apache server, add 'Options -Indexes' to your .htaccess file or server config. For Nginx, ensure the 'autoindex' directive is set to 'off'. Additionally, sensitive files should never be stored in the web root (the folder accessible to the internet) and should require strict authentication to access.

Example Nginx Fix:
server {
    location /uploads/ {
        autoindex off;
    }
}

The server must implement strict authorization checks. Instead of relying on a user-provided email parameter to fetch data, the server should identify the user through a secure, server-side session or a cryptographically signed token (like a JWT) that cannot be tampered with. The backend should verify that the authenticated user's ID matches the ID of the record being requested before returning any data.

// FIXED CODE EXAMPLE (Node.js/Express style)
app.post('/backend/widget/contact/upsert', async (req, res) => {
  const authenticatedUser = req.user; // Get user from secure session/token
  const requestedEmail = req.body.email;

  // Check if the authenticated user has permission to access this email
  if (authenticatedUser.email !== requestedEmail) {
    return res.status(403).send("Unauthorized access");
  }

  // Proceed with upsert logic...
});

First, restrict access to API documentation (like Swagger) so only authenticated developers can see it. Second, implement a strict 'Authorization' check on every single administrative endpoint. The server must verify the user's role before executing any logic. 

Example of fixed code (Pseudo-code):

function handleAdminRequest(request) {
  user = getAuthenticatedUser(request);
  if (user.role != 'ADMIN') {
    return response(403, 'Access Denied');
  }
  // Only proceed if the user is confirmed as an admin
  executeAdminAction();
}

Replace the naïve equality check with a constant‑time comparison that processes the entire secret regardless of mismatches. For example, in many languages use a built‑in function like crypto.timingSafeEqual (Node.js) or subtle.constantTimeCompare (Go). Pseudocode:

function constantTimeEquals($a, $b) {
    $result = 0;
    for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($a); $i++) {
        $result |= ord($a[$i]) ^ ord($b[$i]);
    }
    return $result === 0;
}

This ensures every comparison takes the same amount of time, eliminating the timing leak.

Add a rule to the web server to deny all dot‑files. For Nginx:
```
location ~ /\..* {
    deny all;
    access_log off;
    log_not_found off;
}
```
For Apache:
```
<FilesMatch "^\.">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
```
After deploying the rule, reload the server configuration. Then rotate any secrets that were exposed (APP_KEY, DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD, REDIS_PASSWORD) and set APP_DEBUG=false in production to prevent accidental information leakage.

First, immediately remove any archive or backup files from the web root directory so they cannot be downloaded. Second, configure your web server (like Nginx or Apache) to explicitly deny access to sensitive file extensions like .zip, .sql, and .env. Third, rotate (change) all leaked passwords and encryption keys immediately since they are now compromised. Finally, use a .gitignore file to ensure developers do not accidentally upload secret configuration files to the server.

Example Nginx fix:
location ~ /\.(env|bak|zip|sql) {
    deny all;
}

To fix this, developers should avoid using raw user input to build file paths. Instead, use a unique ID (like a database index) to refer to files. If paths must be used, the application should 'canonicalize' the path (resolve all '../' parts) and verify that the resulting path still resides within the intended directory. 

Example of a secure check in PHP:
$base_dir = '/var/www/html/files/';
$requested_file = realpath($base_dir . $_GET['file']);
if ($requested_file && strpos($requested_file, $base_dir) === 0) {
    // Only serve the file if the resolved path starts with the base directory
    readfile($requested_file);
}

lesson.example:
Imagine a fictional desktop note-taking app called 'NoteMaster' built with Electron. The app allows users to share notes with links. The vulnerable code in the desktop app looks like this: 

const { shell } = require('electron');
function handleLinkClick(userProvidedUrl) {
  shell.openExternal(userProvidedUrl);
}

An attacker creates a note with the following link: 'ms-settings:recovery'. When a victim opens this note in the NoteMaster desktop app and clicks the link, their Windows 'Recovery' settings page immediately pops up. Because 'shell.openExternal' does not check if the link starts with 'https://', it tells the Windows operating system to open whatever the link points to. An attacker could use more dangerous schemes like 'ms-msdt:' to attempt full system takeover.

lesson.fix:
To fix this, developers must implement a strict allowlist for URI schemes before passing any string to system-level APIs. Instead of allowing all links, the code should explicitly check that the URI starts with 'https://' or 'mailto:'. Additionally, when using frameworks like Electron, developers should avoid 'shell.openExternal()' for untrusted input or wrap it in a validation function. 

Example of fixed code:

const { shell } = require('electron');
const allowedSchemes = ['https:', 'http:', 'mailto:'];

function safeOpen(inputUrl) {
  try {
    const parsedUrl = new URL(inputUrl);
    if (allowedSchemes.includes(parsedUrl.protocol)) {
      shell.openExternal(inputUrl);
    } else {
      console.error('Blocked unsafe URI scheme:', parsedUrl.protocol);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Invalid URL');
  }
}

The fix is to implement authorization checks inside every single resolver function, not just at the top-level API gateway. Developers should use a 'context' object to verify the user's identity and permissions before returning any data. Additionally, introspection and field suggestions should be disabled in production environments to prevent attackers from mapping the database. 

Example of fixed code (Node.js):
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    users: (parent, args, context) => {
      if (!context.user || context.user.role !== 'ADMIN') {
        throw new Error('Not authorized');
      }
      return db.Users.findAll();
    }
  }
};

Move all configuration files outside of the public web root directory so they cannot be reached by a URL. If they must remain in that folder, configure the web server to deny all requests to sensitive file extensions. For an Nginx server, add this block to the configuration: location ~* \.(yml|yaml|env|conf)$ { deny all; }. Additionally, never hardcode passwords; use a secret management tool or Docker Secrets to inject credentials at runtime without saving them in the file.

# Example Nginx Fix:
server {
    location ~* (docker-compose\.yml|\.env) {
        deny all;
        return 404;
    }
}

To fix this, administrators must apply granular IAM roles instead of broad predefined roles. Specifically, the 'allUsers' entity should never be granted the 'storage.objects.list' permission. Instead, use a custom role or the 'Storage Object Viewer' role only on specific objects, or use 'Legacy Object Reader' which allows reading but not listing. In Google Cloud, the configuration should look like this:

{
  "bindings": [
    {
      "role": "roles/storage.objectViewer",
      "members": ["user:admin@example.com"]
    },
    {
      "role": "roles/storage.legacyObjectReader",
      "members": ["allUsers"]
    }
  ]
}

Set a strong, non‑empty WAGTAILTRANSFER_SECRET_KEY in production and rotate it regularly. Enforce authentication on the /wagtail-transfer/ endpoint, rejecting unauthenticated requests before HMAC verification. Additionally, limit the models that can be exported and apply rate‑limiting. Example fix in settings.py:
WAGTAILTRANSFER_SECRET_KEY = os.getenv('WAGTAILTRANSFER_SECRET_KEY')
# Ensure the key is at least 32 random bytes
if not WAGTAILTRANSFER_SECRET_KEY:
 raise ImproperlyConfigured('WAGTAILTRANSFER_SECRET_KEY must be set')

In the view:
if not request.user.is_staff:
 return HttpResponseForbidden()
# then verify HMAC as before

lesson.example:
**Fictional vulnerable service – target.example**
Endpoint: POST https://target.example/api/check-email
Request body (JSON): {"email":"alice@target.example"}
Vulnerable code (pseudo‑Python):
```
if db.email_exists(request.json["email"]):
    return {"code": "EXISTS"}
else:
    return {"code": "FREE"}
```
Attack: Change the email value to any address and send the request.
Example request: POST https://target.example/api/check-email {"email":"bob@target.example"}
Response if bob exists: {"code":"EXISTS"}
Response if bob does not exist: {"code":"FREE"}
Why it works: The server returns different codes for the two cases, leaking the existence of the account. A beginner can repeat the same steps with a browser proxy to enumerate users.

Imagine a smart home app 'target.example' that uses a deep link to set the 'Update Server' address: 'smarthome://update?server=updates.target.example'. If the app exports the activity handling this link without validation, a malicious app can send: 'smarthome://update?server=evil-hacker.com'. When the smart home app checks for updates, it will connect to the hacker's server instead of the real one. 

Attack Intent:
Intent i = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse("smarthome://update?server=evil-hacker.com"));
startActivity(i);

Why it works: The app trusts the 'server' parameter from the deep link without checking if it belongs to 'target.example', allowing an attacker to intercept the app's requests or provide malicious update files.

lesson.fix:
To fix this, implement two layers of defense. First, use a 'state' token: when the app starts a login, generate a random, unguessable string and store it in memory. Only accept callbacks that include this exact token. Second, implement a strict allowlist for the 'fqdn' parameter, ensuring it matches a known-good domain exactly.

```java
// Example Fix
String receivedState = uri.getQueryParameter("state");
if (receivedState == null || !receivedState.equals(storedState)) {
    throw new SecurityException("Invalid state token");
}
String fqdn = uri.getQueryParameter("fqdn");
if (!"vpn.vendor.com".equals(fqdn)) {
    throw new SecurityException("Untrusted host");
}
```

To fix this, developers must ensure that the security layer (WAF/Proxy) and the application server interpret URLs in the exact same way. The most robust solution is to implement 'Zero Trust' authentication, where the application itself checks for a valid user session on every sensitive endpoint, rather than relying on the WAF to block paths. If matrix variables (semicolons in paths) are not required, they should be globally disabled. In Spring Boot, you can do this by setting 'setRemoveSemicolonContent(true)' in the configuration: 

@Configuration
public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
    @Override
    public void configurePathMatch(PathMatchConfigurer configurer) {
        UrlPathHelper urlPathHelper = new UrlPathHelper();
        urlPathHelper.setRemoveSemicolonContent(true);
        configurer.setUrlPathHelper(urlPathHelper);
    }
}

lesson.example:
Imagine a fictional site target.example with a 'Forgot Password' page. When you enter an email, the code looks like this:

if (database.hasEmail(inputEmail)) {
  sendEmail(inputEmail);
  return "Check your inbox!";
} else {
  return "That email is not in our system.";
}

An attacker sends this request:
POST /forgot-password HTTP/1.1
email=target@example.com

If the response is "Check your inbox!", the attacker knows that specific person uses the site. This is a 'behavior' called user enumeration. To turn this into a 'bug,' the attacker would need to find a way to bypass the email requirement or use this information to cause harm, such as by chaining it with a password reset vulnerability that doesn't require access to the email inbox.

lesson.fix:
To fix this, the application should return a generic, identical message for all authentication failures, regardless of whether the username exists. This prevents attackers from guessing valid accounts. Additionally, the server should ensure that the time it takes to respond is consistent (preventing timing attacks).

Fixed Code Example:

if (database.hasEmail(inputEmail)) {
  sendEmail(inputEmail);
}
// Always return the same message
return "If that account exists, a reset link has been sent to your email.";

The fix must be implemented on the server-side, not the client-side. Before the server retrieves any data from the database or sends a response, it must verify the user's session and permissions. If the user is not authorized, the server should return a 403 Forbidden or 404 Not Found status code with a completely empty body or a generic error message. 

Example of fixed server-side logic (Node.js/Express):
app.get('/api/get-users', (req, res) => {
  if (!req.user || !req.user.isAdmin) {
    return res.status(404).send({ error: 'Not Found' }); // Send generic error, NO DATA
  }
  const users = db.getAllUsers();
  res.json(users);
});

To fix this, the AES key must be removed from the frontend JavaScript and stored securely on the server. More importantly, the 2FA verification state must be managed entirely on the server side using secure sessions. The server should never trust a client-provided value to determine if a security check was passed. Instead of the client sending an encrypted 'Y', the server should update a database or session variable once the 2FA code is verified. 

Example of secure server-side check:
if (submitted_code === correct_code) {
  req.session.is_2fa_verified = true;
  return res.status(200).send('Success');
}

lesson.example:
Imagine a fictional site called 'DigitalArtStore.example'. When you click on a painting to see its price, the website sends a request to get the painting's details. 

Vulnerable Code (Server-side):
function getProductDetails(productId) {
  const product = db.find(productId);
  // VULNERABILITY: Generating the download link for EVERYONE who views the product page
  const downloadUrl = s3.generatePresignedUrl(product.filePath, { expires: 3600 });
  return { name: product.name, price: product.price, url: downloadUrl };
}

Attack Request:
POST /graphql
Content: { "query": "{ product(id: 50) { name price url } }" }

Response:
{ "data": { "product": { "name": "Sunset.jpg", "price": "$50.00", "url": "https://s3.example/Sunset.jpg?sig=abc123..." } } }

Why it works: The server generates the 'url' (the secret download link) and sends it to the user's browser before checking if the user has actually paid the $50.00. The attacker simply copies the 'url' from the background data and downloads the image for free.

lesson.fix:
Add a server‑side ownership check before returning data. Pseudo‑code fix:
function getUser(req) {
    var id = req.body.account_no;
    var caller = req.session.userId; // authenticated user
    if (caller !== id) {
        return {status:false, error:"Unauthorized"}; // HTTP 403
    }
    var user = database.findById(id);
    return {status:true, data:user};
}
Also ensure that authentication is required (return 401 if no session) and that sensitive fields like tokens are never exposed in public endpoints. Validate the parameter type and length to prevent injection attacks.

To fix this, move all sensitive API credentials to a backend server. The frontend should never hold secrets; instead, it should authenticate to your own backend, which then communicates with the third-party API. Additionally, upgrade OTPs to at least 6 digits to increase the number of combinations to 1,000,000, and implement strict rate limiting (e.g., block an IP or account after 5 failed attempts). Finally, implement Object-Level Authorization to ensure that a session ID can only access data belonging to that specific user.

// Example of a secure backend proxy approach:
// Instead of the browser calling the API with a secret, it calls your server:
router.post('/get-token', async (req, res) => {
  // The secret is stored safely in server environment variables
  const secret = process.env.API_SECRET;
  const token = await callExternalAPI(secret);
  res.json({ token });
});
